<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ilipa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/206-bce">206 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Ilipa was fought in 206 BCE near Ilipa, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. The Roman general Publius Cornelius Scipio, later called Africanus, outmanoeuvred a larger Carthaginian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal Gisco</a> and Mago by reversing his usual battle formation, then routed it. The victory ended Carthaginian power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, and Gadir went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ilipa.jpg" alt="Battle of Ilipa" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Citypeek · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilipa2.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Carthaginian defeat at the Battle of Baecula and the departure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-barca">Hasdrubal Barca</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, new reinforcements reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> at the beginning of 207 BCE under Hanno, who joined Mago Barca, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s younger brother. Scipio sent a detachment under Marcus Junius Silanus against Mago, whose camp was attacked by surprise and scattered, and Hanno was captured. In 206 BCE Hasdrubal Gisco and Mago, still reeling from a series of defeats, joined their Iberian and Numidian allies at Ilipa, near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. They assembled about 70,000 infantry, 4,000 cavalry and 32 elephants. Scipio fielded 45,000 infantry and 4,000 cavalry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Mago attacked the Roman camp with most of the cavalry, including King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Massinissa</a>'s Numidian horsemen, but the Roman cavalry charged his flank. For several days both armies lined up in the same formation, Scipio with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> in the centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> on the flanks, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> mirroring him with their elite African infantry in the centre. Then Scipio prepared his troops overnight, and at dawn sent the velites and cavalry against the enemy camp. The Carthaginians came out in disorder, many still asleep, and took their usual positions. Scipio had reversed his formation, placing the legionaries on the flanks and the Iberians in the centre.</p><p>The cavalries clashed, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> in the Carthaginian line, and the velites used javelins and trumpets to drive the elephants into a frenzy, so that they trampled much of the Carthaginian cavalry. Scipio's Iberians in the centre held back, and the African infantry could not help their comrades without exposing their own centre. They lost heart and began an orderly retreat, which became a rout when the Iberians pursued. The army was virtually annihilated, and Hasdrubal and Mago took refuge in Gades.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>That night Iberian mercenaries deserted the Carthaginian camp, and Hasdrubal tried to slip away in the dark. Scipio pursued and left him with 6,000 men on a mountain top without water, who soon surrendered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal Gisco</a> went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> to visit the Numidian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>, where Scipio also met him, and Mago fled to the Balearics and sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> to attempt an invasion of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Scipio returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and was elected consul in 205 BCE. Many regard Ilipa as his most brilliant victory. It permanently broke the Carthaginian grip on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, denied any further land invasion of Italy and cut off the Barca family's source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and manpower.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ilipa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Scipio captured Qart Hadasht in 209 BCE and won at <b>Ilipa</b> in 206 BCE, ending Carthaginian rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 206 BCE, after the Carthaginian defeat at <b>Ilipa</b>, Gadir went over to Rome under a treaty of alliance.</blockquote><a href="/state/gadir">Gadir</a></li></ul></section>
